`import { defineConfig } from '@tanstack/start/config';
import type { Router } from 'vinxi/dist/types/lib/router-mode';
import tsConfigPaths from 'vite-tsconfig-paths';
const app = defineConfig({
deployment: { preset: 'node-server' },
vite: {
plugins: () => [
tsConfigPaths({
projects: ['./tsconfig.json'],
}),
],
},
});
app.config.server.experimental = { websocket: true, asyncContext: true };
app.addRouter({
name: 'websocket',
type: 'http',
handler: './app/ws.ts',
target: 'server',
base: '/_ws',
} as Router);
app.addRouterPlugins(
({ name }) => name === 'websocket',
() => [
tsConfigPaths({
projects: ['./tsconfig.json'],
}),
],
);
export default app;`

# Docker compose with tanstack start #3147 - GitHub
created: 2025-02-05, last updated: 2025-10-28, [Read more](https://github.com/TanStack/router/discussions/3147)

It seems that vinxi doesn't allow you to configure the underlying vite hmr options needed to get this to work in Docker. I suspect I need to set `vite.server.hmr.host` to `'0.0.0.0'` and/or `vite.server.hmr.port` to a static port that's mapped in the Docker compose file. nksaraf/vinxi#394 is an FR to allow HMR ports to be configurable.
...
#### timothyaveni May 15, 2025
-
I was able to configure this with
app.config.ts
```
export default defineConfig({
  ..
  routers: {
    client: {
      vite: {
        server: { // gives a ts error, but still seems to work
          hmr: {
            port: 3124
          }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  ...
})
```
...
nksaraf/vinxi#455
So if you're using devcontainers in vscode, you can help vscode autoforward the ports for you by making vite report the ports it's using for HMR.
